;;; hash-table-update-value-default.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples
;;;
;;; A broken-example is kept ON PURPOSE (CLAUDE.md § Broken-examples teach
;;; the differences). This is the counter/histogram version of the
;;; hash-table-ref failure-thunk trap: the seed argument to `hash-table-update!`
;;; is a THUNK under strict SRFI-125, not a plain starting value. The classic
;;; "start counts at 0" idiom passes a bare 0 and raises on the first unseen key.
;;;
;;; Provenance for the DECISION: Motoi conformed `hash-table-update!` to strict
;;; SRFI-125 on 2026-07-24. The behavior-preserving fix for a constant seed is
;;; `hash-table-update!/default`, whose fourth argument IS a plain value.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — hash-table-update! seed arg is a thunk, not a value")

(broken-example :name "hash-table-update!"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-update-failure-thunk-contract"
  :std-target "SRFI-125"
  :std-rejected ("SRFI-69-value-seed-reading")
  :status "conformed"
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme/reference/broken-examples/hash-table-update-value-default.slatl"

  ;; ─── THE BROKEN CODE (raises on the first missing key) ───
  :broken-code "(define counts (make-hash-table))\n(hash-table-update! counts 'apple (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0)\n(display (hash-table-ref/default counts 'apple 'none))\n(newline)"
  :broken-output "error: not a function: 0"
  :runs "no — the seed 0 is called as a thunk on the missing key"

  ;; ─── WHY IT IS BROKEN ───
  :why
"SRFI-125 specifies `(hash-table-update! h k updater [failure [success]])`
where `failure` is a THUNK of no arguments producing the value to seed when k
is absent. Motoi's engine follows the spec: on a miss it CALLS the fourth
argument to obtain the seed, then applies the updater to it. A bare `0` is not
callable, so `(hash-table-update! counts 'apple (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0)`
raises `not a function: 0` the moment `apple` is first counted.

Unlike the ref trap, there is no hidden-hit escape here: the histogram idiom
ALWAYS starts from an absent key, so the wrong form fails on the very first
increment. That makes it easier to catch — but it silently breaks every
counter, tally, and group-by written against the old value-seed reading."

  ;; ─── THE FIX ───
  :fix-code "(define counts (make-hash-table))\n(hash-table-update!/default counts 'apple (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0)\n(display (hash-table-ref/default counts 'apple 'none))\n(newline)"
  :fix-output "1"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note
"When the missing-key seed is a constant, use `hash-table-update!/default` —
its fourth argument IS a plain value. When the seed must be computed lazily or
should raise on a miss, keep `hash-table-update!` and pass an actual thunk:
`(hash-table-update! h k updater (lambda () (compute-seed)))`."

  ;; ─── THE LESSON ───
  :lesson
"The counter idiom `(update! h k inc 0)` is the single most common hash-table
pattern, and it is exactly the one strict SRFI-125 rejects with a bare seed.
The fourth argument of `hash-table-update!` is a THUNK; the fourth argument of
`hash-table-update!/default` is a VALUE. Histograms, tallies, and word-frequency
counters all want the `/default` form. See book-of-r7rs-recipes ch03 § Problem 2."

  ;; ─── THE SAME IDEA IN THREE OTHER LIBRARIES ───
  :three-other-libs (
    (:lib "SRFI-125"
     :status "hash-table-update! failure arg is a thunk; /default takes a value"
     :code "(hash-table-update!/default h k (lambda (v) (+ v 1)) 0)"
     :note "The target contract. The /default variant is the histogram spelling.")
    (:lib "SRFI-69"
     :status "hash-table-update!/default identical; update! also thunk-seeded"
     :code "(hash-table-update!/default h k 1+ 0)"
     :note "Stable across SRFI-69 and SRFI-125; the /default form is the portable one.")
    (:lib "Python"
     :status "collections.defaultdict(int) or dict.get(k, 0) supplies the value seed directly"
     :code "counts[k] = counts.get(k, 0) + 1"
     :note "Python's get takes a VALUE default; the mental model of a plain seed is exactly what mis-ports to Motoi's thunk-seeded update!.")
  )

  :motoi-standard
"Target: strict SRFI-125 `hash-table-update!` (fourth arg = failure thunk,
absent = raise) PLUS `hash-table-update!/default` (fourth arg = value seed).
Conformed in the engine 2026-07-24. Corpus counter/histogram sites migrated to
`/default`. Kept as the counter-example that teaches the seed thunk-vs-value
distinction.")
